From 879a46c15fc3b7f00f509516148d91d2fc31de97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Giedr=C4=97=20=C5=A0imk=C5=ABnait=C4=97?=
 <giedre.s@8devices.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:47:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] devicetree: document virtual-thermal-zones driver binding.
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ref.: 8devices/software/yocto/meta-8dev-qcs405#799

The binding listed the properties but not the behaviour a device tree
author has to know in order to use them safely.  Several of the traps
are silent at probe and only show up as a zone that does not throttle.

Document what the driver actually does:

 - A source may itself be a virtual zone, so zones can be chained.
   Dependency cycles and chains deeper than eight are rejected.
 - 'calculate' is ignored for a single-source zone, and is the one
   string property matched case-sensitively - unlike 'thermal-governor'
   and a trip's 'type', which the kernel matches case insensitively.  A
   misspelling therefore falls back to 'max', the least forgiving
   method, which is the opposite of what someone asking for 'median'
   wanted.
 - 'rolling-average' stops filtering altogether once 'polling-delay'
   exceeds forty times 'sample-interval-ms': the window is restarted on
   every ordinary poll and never holds more than one sample.
 - A zero 'polling-delay-passive' is overridden only when
   'polling-delay' is non-zero.  With both at zero the zone is never
   polled, and its trips are evaluated only when something else prompts
   an update - not on a plain read of 'temp', and not on a 'policy'
   write.
 - A correction large enough to saturate the reported temperature reads
   above every positive trip, a 'critical' one included, and a critical
   trip powers the board off rather than driving a cooling device.

Also record what cannot be checked ahead of time: these nodes live
under /thermal-zones, which thermal-zones.yaml claims with
additionalProperties: false, so dt_binding_check never sees them and a
typo is caught only by the driver at probe.

Signed-off-by: Giedrė Šimkūnaitė <giedre.s@8devices.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 4c4dd6cecde8cee5c59bb15af55287c67e76fff7)
Upstream-Status: Pending

---
 .../thermal/virtual-thermal-zone.yaml         | 432 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 410 insertions(+), 22 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/virtual-thermal-zone.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/virtual-thermal-zone.yaml
index e902c2615a18..9edc6d97b9d0 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/virtual-thermal-zone.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/thermal/virtual-thermal-zone.yaml
@@ -30,6 +30,143 @@ description: |
   own platform device and automatically discovers all virtual thermal zone
   definitions.
 
+  A source may be a real thermal zone or another virtual zone. The driver
+  resolves the dependency graph from the device tree before registering
+  anything and registers zones in order of increasing depth, so a source
+  always exists before the zones that consume it; the order of the nodes in
+  the device tree does not matter. Dependency cycles, including a zone that
+  lists itself, and chains more than 8 zones deep are rejected.
+
+  At most 32 virtual zones are registered. Any beyond that are dropped with a
+  message, in device tree order. A zone that names a dropped one as a source is
+  not rejected outright: the driver cannot tell it apart from a real zone that
+  has yet to register, so it waits out the deferred-probe timeout before
+  skipping it.
+
+  The thermal core rejects a zone name of 20 characters or more outright, so a
+  virtual zone node name must be at most 19 characters. The driver drops a
+  longer one with a message rather than let registration fail for it.
+
+  Zone names are matched case-insensitively, so two nodes whose names differ
+  only in case are one zone as far as the framework is concerned. Both
+  still register and both still work, but neither can then be named in another
+  zone's source-thermal-zones - the lookup matches both and fails - and
+  userspace that identifies a zone by its type cannot tell them apart. The
+  driver warns about this too.
+
+  The cost falls on the consumer rather than on the colliding pair. A zone
+  naming a collided name as a source cannot tell -EEXIST from a source that
+  has not registered yet, so it defers for the whole deferred-probe window
+  and is then skipped with a message about the source not being registered -
+  which points at the wrong thing. A collision between a virtual zone and a
+  sensor-backed one also breaks that real zone for every other in-kernel user
+  of its name, not just for this driver.
+
+  A virtual zone node must not carry 'thermal-sensors'. thermal_of claims a
+  zone on that property alone and never looks at 'compatible', so a node with
+  both would be registered twice - once reading the sensor and once aggregating
+  - under one name. The driver ignores such a node and says so.
+
+  IMPORTANT - where these nodes may be placed
+
+    Put every virtual zone node AFTER all sensor-backed zones under
+    /thermal-zones. A sensor whose zone appears after a virtual zone in device
+    tree order does not get registered at all: its real thermal zone silently
+    fails to appear, and the driver cannot prevent that.
+
+  A virtual zone node deliberately has no 'thermal-sensors' property, and
+  thermal_of does not tolerate such a child. of_thermal_zone_find() in Linux 6.6
+  walks every available child of /thermal-zones looking for the zone that
+  belongs to the sensor being registered, and on the first child without
+  'thermal-sensors' it logs "<node>: missing thermal sensor" and abandons the
+  entire search instead of skipping that node. Every sensor whose own zone had
+  not been reached yet therefore loses its zone.
+
+  Adding the nodes with &{/thermal-zones} { ... }, which is how a board normally
+  adds them, appends them and is safe. Declaring a virtual zone ahead of the
+  SoC's own zones - in a .dtsi included before the SoC one, or by deleting and
+  re-adding a zone - is not.
+
+  A sensor that has no zone at all is a related, harmless but misleading case:
+  its search runs off the end of the list and logs that message against the
+  first virtual zone it reaches, which had nothing to do with it.
+
+  This is a thermal_of limitation, not something this binding or the driver can
+  guard against; the nodes are already in the list by the time thermal_of walks
+  it.
+
+  The driver becomes a device-link consumer of the platform device behind each
+  real source zone, so unbinding a sensor driver unbinds this driver first and
+  every virtual zone disappears with it, rather than being left pointing at
+  freed zones.
+
+  Note what "every" means there. This driver registers one platform device for
+  the whole board, so every device link is consumer-side from that single
+  device: unbinding any one sensor driver takes down all of the virtual zones,
+  including ones that never named that sensor as a source. They come back when
+  the provider rebinds, but they are registered afresh - filter state is lost,
+  and the thermal_zoneN numbers come from an ID allocator, so
+  /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zoneN paths are not stable across an unbind and
+  rebind. Anything that remembers a zone by path rather than by 'type' has to
+  cope with that.
+
+  The probe is also all or nothing: if any zone has to wait for a source, the
+  whole set is rolled back and retried. Three consequences worth planning for.
+  Built into the kernel, one source that is described in the device tree but
+  never registers holds back every virtual zone until the deferred-probe
+  timeout expires - ten seconds by default - so nothing that enumerates
+  thermal zones early in boot will see them. Loaded as a module after boot,
+  there is no grace period at all: deferred probing has already ended, so any
+  source not already registered at modprobe time is dropped on the first
+  attempt rather than waited for. And a source whose provider binds later does
+  not bring its zone back, because a source that could not be linked leaves
+  nothing behind to trigger a re-probe; that needs a manual unbind and bind of
+  this driver.
+
+  One kind of source cannot be tied to the driver this way: one whose provider
+  sits on a bus other than the platform bus, such as an SPMI, I2C or SPI sensor,
+  because the provider is looked up on the platform bus only. The driver warns
+  for each such source. For those the zone is safe until that sensor driver is
+  unbound and unsafe from then on, so avoid building a virtual zone on a sensor
+  whose driver is expected to be unbound at runtime.
+
+  If none of a zone's sources can be read the temperature read fails, and the
+  thermal core then leaves the zone's last good temperature in place and keeps
+  driving the trips from it. A zone whose sources have all died therefore
+  freezes at its last reading rather than reporting a failure. The driver logs
+  this, rate limited, with a count of consecutive failed reads.
+
+  The core keeps its own message quiet. update_temperature() warns once per
+  poll, unlimited, for any read failure that is not -EAGAIN - which on a zone
+  whose sources had all died would mean one line per poll, at whichever of the
+  two delays is in force ('polling-delay', unless a governor has put the zone
+  in the passive regime, and only step_wise and power_allocator ever do that),
+  for as long as they stayed dead. The driver returns -EAGAIN for exactly this
+  reason, so its own rate-limited line - which carries a count of consecutive
+  failed reads - is the only report you get.
+
+  A partial failure is the opposite, and quieter than it should be: a source
+  that cannot be read is dropped from the aggregate with only a pr_debug, and
+  the zone keeps reporting a plausible temperature computed from whichever
+  sources still answer. With 'max' that reading can only be too low, and the
+  trips that depend on it stop firing, with nothing in the log to say why.
+
+  A virtual zone registers with 'no_hwmon' set, so unlike a sensor-backed zone
+  it gets no /sys/class/hwmon entry. It is readable through
+  /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zoneN/temp like any other zone.
+
+  Validation
+
+    These nodes do not pass 'make dt_binding_check' or 'make dtbs_check', and
+    cannot be made to without changing thermal-zones.yaml. That schema owns
+    every child of /thermal-zones: it requires 'thermal-sensors', 'trips',
+    'polling-delay' and 'polling-delay-passive' on each one, sets
+    additionalProperties: false so 'compatible' and 'source-thermal-zones' are
+    rejected, and restricts node names to the form '<name>-thermal'. A virtual
+    zone contradicts the first two by design. This is a tooling gap rather than
+    a defect in the device tree: the kernel parses these nodes exactly as
+    described here.
+
 properties:
   compatible:
     const: virtual-thermal-zone
@@ -37,8 +174,19 @@ properties:
   source-thermal-zones:
     $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/string-array
     description: |
-      Array of thermal zone names to aggregate. The driver will look up these
-      thermal zones by name and read their temperatures.
+      Array of thermal zone names to aggregate. The driver looks these zones
+      up by name and reads their temperatures. A name may refer to a real
+      thermal zone or to another virtual zone; see the chaining rules above.
+
+      Names are matched the way the thermal core matches them: case
+      insensitively and in full. There is no prefix matching - an
+      abbreviated or over-long name resolves to nothing. Listing the same
+      zone more than once makes the averaging methods count it more than
+      once; the driver warns about it but does not reject it.
+
+      The 32-entry cap is a schema limit. The driver does not enforce it and
+      sizes every allocation from the actual count, so a longer list works;
+      it simply is not valid against this binding.
     minItems: 1
     maxItems: 32
 
@@ -64,9 +212,31 @@ properties:
       - average: Arithmetic mean of all sources
       - median: Median value of all sources
       - mean: Same as average
-      - weighted-average: Weighted average (currently equal weights)
-      - rolling-average: Moving average over last 5 readings
-      - exponential-average: Exponential moving average (alpha=0.3)
+      - weighted-average: Mean weighted by 'source-weights'. A source that
+        fails to read is dropped together with its weight, so the remaining
+        weights stay matched to their zones
+      - rolling-average: Boxcar average over the last 5 samples, one sample
+        per 'sample-interval-ms'. See that property for what a long gap
+        between reads does to the window
+      - exponential-average: Exponential moving average with a time constant
+        of 'time-constant-ms'. The smoothing factor is derived from the
+        elapsed time, so the result does not depend on how often the zone is
+        read
+
+      Those names are matched exactly, case included - unlike
+      'thermal-governor' and a trip's 'type', both of which the kernel matches
+      case insensitively. Any other value, a differing case among them, is
+      reported in the log and the zone falls back to 'max'. That fallback is
+      worth knowing about rather than discovering: 'max' is the least
+      forgiving of these methods, where one sensor reading high carries the
+      zone on its own, which is the opposite of what someone asking for
+      'median' wanted. (A single-source zone never reaches this: it ignores
+      the property altogether, as above.)
+
+      The two filtering methods combine the sources before they filter: each
+      sample is the plain average of the sources that could be read, and it is
+      that average which the boxcar or the exponential moving average is
+      applied to. They do not filter each source separately.
     enum:
       - max
       - min
@@ -77,17 +247,113 @@ properties:
       - rolling-average
       - exponential-average
 
+  source-weights:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32-array
+    description: |
+      One weight per entry of 'source-thermal-zones', used by
+      calculate = "weighted-average". Each weight is at most 65535; a zone
+      naming a larger one is rejected, not clamped.
+
+      If the property is absent every source weighs the same. If the weights
+      of the sources that could be read sum to zero, the plain average is
+      used instead. Any other calculation method ignores the property and
+      the driver warns about it.
+    items:
+      maximum: 65535
+    minItems: 1
+    maxItems: 32
+
+  time-constant-ms:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+    description: |
+      Time constant of calculate = "exponential-average", in milliseconds.
+      For a step of dt milliseconds the smoothing factor is
+      dt / (time-constant-ms + dt). A gap longer than eight time constants
+      reseeds the average rather than integrating across it.
+
+      The arithmetic is integer, so the average settles to within roughly
+      (time-constant-ms + dt) / dt millidegrees of the input rather than
+      converging exactly - a few millidegrees at the default, far below what a
+      sensor resolves.
+
+      The bounds below describe a valid device tree. A value outside them is
+      clamped into range by the driver rather than rejected, so an out-of-spec
+      tree still gets defined behaviour.
+    minimum: 10
+    maximum: 600000
+    default: 3000
+
+  sample-interval-ms:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+    description: |
+      Minimum spacing between samples of calculate = "rolling-average", in
+      milliseconds. A read that arrives sooner returns the previous result
+      unchanged, so the window always spans a known amount of time no matter
+      how often the zone is read. Defaults to the value of 'polling-delay'.
+      As with 'time-constant-ms', a value outside the bounds below is clamped
+      by the driver rather than rejected.
+
+      A gap of more than 40 times this value between two samples restarts the
+      window rather than pushing into it, the way 'time-constant-ms' reseeds
+      the exponential average. Without that, readings taken after an idle
+      period would be blended with temperatures from before it. The zone is
+      briefly unfiltered afterwards: the first reading past the restart is
+      reported raw and the next four are averaged over a window that is still
+      filling.
+
+      That threshold is measured from the last sample the filter accepted, so
+      between samples it is simply how often the zone is read. Setting this
+      property to less than a fortieth of the rate the zone is actually read
+      at therefore restarts the window on every read, leaving one sample in it
+      and making 'rolling-average' behave as a pass-through with no warning.
+      It takes a tree asking for a window shorter than the interval it reads
+      at, which is self-contradictory - with one exception that is easy to
+      reach by accident. Leaving the property out normally avoids the problem,
+      because it then follows 'polling-delay'; but 'polling-delay = <0>' means
+      the zone is read on demand only, and 0 is clamped up to 10, giving a
+      restart threshold of 400 ms. An on-demand zone is almost always read
+      less often than that, so it restarts on every read and does not filter
+      at all. A zone that wants both on-demand reads and a working
+      'rolling-average' must set this property explicitly, to something near
+      the rate it is actually read at.
+    minimum: 10
+    maximum: 600000
+
   polling-delay-passive:
     $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
     description: |
       Polling interval in milliseconds when any trip point is active.
       If not specified, defaults to 100 ms.
 
+      A value of 0 is overridden, unless 'polling-delay' is 0 as well - a zone
+      that asks for no polling at all is left alone, silently, because there
+      is no non-zero delay to fall back to and it is read on demand instead.
+      Nothing polls such a zone, so its trips are evaluated only when
+      something else prompts an update: the zone being enabled, by probe or by
+      a 'mode' write, or a cooling device binding to it. Never on a plain read
+      of 'temp', and not on a 'policy' write either - changing the governor
+      swaps it and re-evaluates nothing.
+
+      With a non-zero 'polling-delay' the override applies, and this is why:
+      the thermal core polls at the passive interval once a governor marks
+      the zone passive and has no fallback to 'polling-delay' there, and a
+      zero interval cancels the poll work outright - after which nothing
+      re-arms it for a zone that has no sensor driver of its own, so the
+      trips would stop being evaluated for good while reads carried on
+      answering. The driver warns and uses 'polling-delay' instead.
+
+      The thermal core takes this as a signed int, so a value above 2147483647
+      arrives negative and msecs_to_jiffies() turns it into MAX_JIFFY_OFFSET,
+      which stops the zone polling altogether.
+    maximum: 2147483647
+
   polling-delay:
     $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
     description: |
       Polling interval in milliseconds when no trip points are active.
-      If not specified, defaults to 1000 ms.
+      If not specified, defaults to 1000 ms. The same signed-int limit as
+      'polling-delay-passive' applies.
+    maximum: 2147483647
 
   sustainable-power:
     $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
@@ -97,14 +363,78 @@ properties:
   coefficients:
     $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32-array
     description: |
-      Thermal coefficients [slope, offset] for temperature calculation.
+      Linear correction <slope offset> applied to the aggregated temperature:
+      the zone reports slope * aggregate + offset, in millidegree Celsius.
+      Defaults to <1 0>, which leaves the aggregate unchanged. Two values are
+      expected; the driver reads the first two and ignores anything after
+      them, though the schema accepts no more than two. The slope must be
+      within +/-65535 and the offset within +/-1000000 (1000 degrees C); a
+      zone outside those bounds is rejected. The corrected value is clamped
+      to the int the thermal core stores, so it cannot overflow.
+
+      Device tree cells are unsigned, so a negative slope or offset is written
+      as its two's-complement value - dtc accepts <(-2000)> for that.
+
+      Three things to know. A longer array is accepted and silently truncated
+      to the first two cells, so the thermal-zones.yaml convention of one
+      coefficient per sensor plus a constant parses here and quietly means
+      something else; the schema forbids it but these nodes cannot be
+      validated. The slope is an integer multiplier, so a gain below 1 cannot
+      be expressed - scale the offset instead, or leave the correction out.
+      And <0 X> is accepted: the zone then reports X for ever, regardless of
+      its sources.
+
+      A slope of zero or below does not disturb how the two filtering methods
+      report their direction to a governor. The driver tracks that direction
+      before the correction is applied and then maps it onto the corrected
+      value: the correction is linear, so a positive slope keeps the
+      direction, a negative slope reverses it, and a zero slope pins the zone
+      to 'offset' and so reports stable. A governor therefore sees the
+      direction of the temperature it is actually comparing against the trip.
+
+      The exception is a correction big enough to reach the clamp mentioned
+      above - a slope of 65535 against a warm aggregate already exceeds the
+      int - where the reported temperature is pinned at the limit while the
+      direction carries on moving. The governor still acts consistently with
+      what the zone reports - it drives cooling up at the top of the range and
+      releases it at the bottom - so the two merely stop agreeing, and a zone
+      corrected that far is no longer measuring anything. How far up it gets
+      depends on the governor: 'bang_bang' goes straight to its cooling state,
+      while 'step_wise' climbs only while the trend reports rising and holds
+      where it is once the trend reads stable.
+
+      One consequence is not merely a disagreement. A zone pinned at the top
+      of the range reads above every positive trip, a 'critical' one included,
+      and a critical trip that fires powers the board off - the thermal core
+      shuts the machine down rather than calling a cooling device. A slope of
+      65535 reaches the clamp at an aggregate of about 33 degrees C, so a
+      correction entered by mistake can take the board down on the next poll.
+      Check the coefficients on any zone that carries a critical trip.
     minItems: 2
     maxItems: 2
 
   thermal-governor:
     $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/string
     description: |
-      Name of the thermal governor to use for this zone.
+      Name of the thermal governor to use for this zone, matched case
+      insensitively. The driver accepts only the governors the running
+      kernel was actually built with. Anything else - a typo, or a real
+      governor this build does not have - makes the driver warn and leave
+      the zone on the kernel's default governor. The zone still registers
+      and still works; it simply is not throttled the way this node asked
+      for.
+
+      That silent fallback is the likely outcome for most of the names
+      below, because a kernel is usually built with only one or two
+      governors while this list names five. Nothing fails at boot, so
+      confirm the governor was taken rather than assuming it: the driver
+      prints the effective governor for every zone it registers, the zone's
+      own sysfs 'policy' attribute reports it on a running system, and
+      /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0/available_policies lists what the
+      kernel has. When writing the tree, the CONFIG_THERMAL_GOV_* symbols
+      for the kernel this tree is for are the same information. The
+      examples below are not a statement that every governor they name is
+      available.
     enum:
       - step_wise
       - fair_share
@@ -116,7 +446,10 @@ properties:
     type: object
     description: |
       Trip points for the virtual thermal zone.
-      Maximum 64 trip points supported (limited by thermal subsystem bitmask).
+      At most 31 trip points; the thermal core rejects a zone with more.
+      A trip whose 'temperature' is 0 is disabled by the thermal core as it
+      registers the zone, without a message, so 0 cannot be used to mean
+      'trip at freezing'.
     patternProperties:
       "^[a-zA-Z0-9\\-_]+$":
         type: object
@@ -131,7 +464,27 @@ properties:
 
           type:
             $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/string
-            description: Trip point type.
+            description: |
+              Trip point type.
+
+              'active' and 'passive' drive the cooling devices bound to the
+              trip through cooling-maps. These are what a virtual zone is
+              normally for.
+
+              'hot' takes no in-kernel action on a virtual zone. The core
+              calls the zone's .hot callback for this type and this driver
+              provides none, and a 'hot' trip never reaches a governor, so it
+              drives no cooling device and does not change the polling window
+              either. What it still does is generate the netlink trip-crossed
+              events the core sends for every trip type, which a userspace
+              thermal daemon can act on. It is accepted; the driver warns.
+
+              'critical' powers the board off: the core installs
+              thermal_zone_device_critical(), which calls
+              hw_protection_shutdown(). Consider the calculation method before
+              putting one on an aggregate - with the default calculate = "max"
+              a single faulty sensor reading high is enough to shut the board
+              down, whereas "median" rejects one outlier.
             enum:
               - active
               - passive
@@ -183,7 +536,7 @@ examples:
   - |
     // Virtual thermal zone definitions
     thermal-zones {
-        cpuss-max-step {
+        cpussmax-thermal {
             compatible = "virtual-thermal-zone";
             source-thermal-zones = "cluster0-thermal", "cluster1-thermal",
                                    "cpu0-thermal", "cpu1-thermal";
@@ -201,16 +554,10 @@ examples:
                     type = "passive";
                 };
 
-                cpuss_hot: cpuss-hot {
+                cpuss_escalate: cpuss-escalate {
                     temperature = <85000>;
                     hysteresis = <5000>;
-                    type = "hot";
-                };
-
-                cpuss_critical: cpuss-critical {
-                    temperature = <95000>;
-                    hysteresis = <0>;
-                    type = "critical";
+                    type = "passive";
                 };
             };
 
@@ -222,14 +569,14 @@ examples:
                 };
 
                 map1 {
-                    trip = <&cpuss_hot>;
+                    trip = <&cpuss_escalate>;
                     cooling-device = <&CPU0 4 8>;
                     contribution = <2048>;
                 };
             };
         };
 
-        gpu-average {
+        gpuavg-thermal {
             compatible = "virtual-thermal-zone";
             source-thermal-zones = "gpu0-thermal", "gpu1-thermal";
             calculate = "average";
@@ -256,7 +603,7 @@ examples:
         // Single-source virtual zone with implicit 'none' calculation method
         // Useful for adding cooling policies to existing thermal zones
         // without modifying the original zone definition
-        wifi-virtual {
+        wifivirt-thermal {
             compatible = "virtual-thermal-zone";
             source-thermal-zones = "wifi-thermal";
             // calculate defaults to 'none' for single source
@@ -279,5 +626,46 @@ examples:
                 };
             };
         };
+
+        // Weighted aggregate: the cluster sensor counts double
+        socwavg-thermal {
+            compatible = "virtual-thermal-zone";
+            source-thermal-zones = "cluster-thermal", "cpu0-thermal",
+                                   "cpu1-thermal";
+            calculate = "weighted-average";
+            source-weights = <2 1 1>;
+            coefficients = <1 0>;
+            polling-delay-passive = <100>;
+            polling-delay = <1000>;
+
+            trips {
+                socwavg_passive: socwavg-passive {
+                    temperature = <80000>;
+                    hysteresis = <5000>;
+                    type = "passive";
+                };
+            };
+        };
+
+        // Chained zone: 'socwavg-thermal' is itself a virtual zone, and is
+        // registered before this one whatever the node order here. The
+        // exponential average is expressed in real time, so reading this
+        // zone from sysfs does not disturb it.
+        socema-thermal {
+            compatible = "virtual-thermal-zone";
+            source-thermal-zones = "socwavg-thermal", "cpu2-thermal";
+            calculate = "exponential-average";
+            time-constant-ms = <5000>;
+            polling-delay-passive = <250>;
+            polling-delay = <1000>;
+
+            trips {
+                socema_passive: socema-passive {
+                    temperature = <90000>;
+                    hysteresis = <5000>;
+                    type = "passive";
+                };
+            };
+        };
     };
 
